and I can speak from experience on that where&nbsp;
I've actually sold out of a startup so I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:28.400
actually go back and fix my home life. There's&nbsp;
the fourth one though is a very interesting one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:32.120
which is when you're in a position and&nbsp;
you start to end up... You end getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:36.600
stretched into other places where you're not&nbsp;
necessarily so good, but the fact is it is part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:41.000
of the experience, but you need to be realigned.
It's like help me realign back to the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:46.200
I'm really good at and what I like to do.&nbsp;
And so ultimately you go into a position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:50.320
you get a promotion, you end up moving to&nbsp;
a place where you can see where it's there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:54.360
and all of a sudden you end up waking&nbsp;
up one morning and going like, God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:59.400
why am I doing all this stuff? I really like&nbsp;
to do that stuff. I had this one, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:05.600
I grew my firm where I had almost 50 people&nbsp;
and I started to realize I spent all my time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:10.160
on people issues and I love to work on product.&nbsp;
And so eventually I actually reshaped the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:15.600
whole business to get us down to five people.
I found everybody else jobs and then ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:19.440
helped me basically get back to being able to&nbsp;
do the work because that's the stuff I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:23.960
to do. And so it's these four different quests&nbsp;
that you realize. And what's interesting is if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:28.840
I look through my career, I've been in all&nbsp;
four of those quests sometime in my life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:34.440
and part of it is to assess your situation so&nbsp;
you understand what quest you're on so you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:39.120
actually start to understand what it's going&nbsp;
to take to make the move that you need to make.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:43.160
That was the beginning of the question I was about&nbsp;
to ask, which is why this is so important. So why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:47.800
does someone need to spend time understanding&nbsp;
what is pulling them to get out to leave?

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:54.920
If I go back to Jobs to Done theory, its value&nbsp;
is created by the context that you're in and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.920 --> 00:15:00.080
outcome that you want and you start to realize&nbsp;
that through your life, your context changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:04.240
And so at some point, for example, in my twenties,&nbsp;
I'd love to learn a lot of things. And so it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:07.720
this whole notion of learning lots of things.&nbsp;
But once I mastered it was like, all right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:13.320
I know all these things. What can I do next? And&nbsp;
so you start to realize that it's about that.&nbsp;

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:17.920
Where are you in this world and why is it&nbsp;
creating that space for you to go like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:23.880
"Yeah, what else can I do?" And what I would say&nbsp;
is nobody randomly changes jobs, it's just not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:29.120
possible. And so the reality is you can describe&nbsp;
it that way and most people would say Lucky is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:33.400
random, but the reality is it's caused and&nbsp;
if it's now caused, you start to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:38.440
realize that context has a lot of impact in&nbsp;
it because if I don't have enough context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:43.400
I don't have enough pushes, I can complain&nbsp;
about my job, but I'll never make the switch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:46.760
So how many people say they want a new job,&nbsp;
but they actually don't know what to do?

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:49.600
So many people talk about I out of here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.440
I've got to leave, I got to go.&nbsp;
And then they never do. That's

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:56.960
Right. And so what you start to realize&nbsp;
is I have four kids and my daughter would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.960 --> 00:16:01.560
come home one day and I'd say, how is work?&nbsp;
And if she could name me four of the pushes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:06.560
I knew she was already looking for another job.&nbsp;
And so it's this notion of it's not anyone push,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:12.480
but it's when the set of pushes come together and&nbsp;
really give you enough energy to go like, yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:17.960
I can't see where to go. I am disrespected. And&nbsp;
the fact is I'm not learning as much as I used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:22.520
learn, I got to go find a new place. And so it's&nbsp;
these three or four things that have to happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:27.680
that ultimately cause people to say, today's&nbsp;
the day I got to leave. But the other part is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:32.920
they won't leave if they don't know where to go.
And so a lot of people end up, they have enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:37.240
energy to leave, but then they actually just&nbsp;
go get another job in the same position they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:42.120
had before and that it's actually worse, the same&nbsp;
or worse than it was the other place. And so part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:45.680
of this is that you have to understand what does&nbsp;
progress mean to you? So we have to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:50.040
the polls, which are these things that happen to&nbsp;
you that the outcomes that you're actually seeking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:55.560
by going to this job. And it's not more money.&nbsp;
Like 53% of the people who basically said they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.560 --> 00:17:00.720
got more money, did not get more money. They&nbsp;
told the company they had more money because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:05.320
they knew they couldn't argue with having them&nbsp;
come back because they got more money. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.320 --> 00:17:09.920
their mind they're like, if they give me that&nbsp;
much money, I'll stay. And it's like it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:14.560
really the reason. And so this is where you start&nbsp;
to peel back that onion and you realize it's very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:20.600
very fertile in terms of all this energy&nbsp;
to cause people to make a job change.

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:24.000
I want to go back to people looking for&nbsp;
a job right now, but before we do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:28.840
you talked about energy drivers, energy drains.&nbsp;
This is such an important and fertile area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:32.320
and you have an actual guide for helping&nbsp;
you figure out what gives you energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:35.160
what drains your energy so that you&nbsp;
can find work that gives you energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:39.600
which is really important, share advice&nbsp;
on how to figure this out for yourself.

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:43.280
One of the things we do in the book, so it's&nbsp;
nine steps for you to basically go through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:47.840
And the reality is I would tell you, I'm not&nbsp;
sure anybody's going to do all nine steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:52.440
but the fact is this is like if you're going&nbsp;
to do this, this is the best of the best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:56.600
but if you do five of the steps, you're going&nbsp;
to actually be way better off. So it's like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.440
don't want to intimidate people by nine steps,&nbsp;
but the reality is this is a really big one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:05.720
which is to go back through your career and even&nbsp;
back to college, I've taken people back to high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:12.680
school and basically talk about those moments&nbsp;
where you got energy, you walked into a situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.680 --> 00:18:16.880
and you literally were like, "Oh my God, I got so&nbsp;
excited about this and =why did I get excited?"&nbsp;

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:20.960
It's one, capturing those moments and&nbsp;
then dissecting those moments to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:25.680
what was it about that context that gave you&nbsp;
energy that made you excited, right? For me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:28.960
it was like, oh, learning something&nbsp;
new. I get really excited by learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:33.920
new things. And so it's like, okay, and what I&nbsp;
realized is that when I actually know something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.880
I actually get bored with it. It actually sucks&nbsp;
my energy because then I got to prove myself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:42.360
everybody else as opposed to trying to learn&nbsp;
something new. The other half is these energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:47.800
drains, which is this aspect of the things that&nbsp;
you go in and just suck the life out of you. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:53.440
what I started to realize is everybody's got to&nbsp;
do some work that sucks their energy. I got to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:57.960
expense reports, there's just certain things&nbsp;
I have to do, PNL, state, all these things.&nbsp;

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:04.560
But the reality is most people spend 95% of&nbsp;
their time doing the work that sucks their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:09.840
energy so they get the 5% of the joy of the work&nbsp;
they do. And what I realized is that if you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:16.000
pull that ratio to 40% energy drive or drain&nbsp;
or 50 50, you don't even know you're working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:20.720
anymore because you're just used to basically&nbsp;
always doing the grind to get where you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:25.720
to go. And so part of this is to realize that&nbsp;
it's about reducing stress as well, but energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:30.920
drivers and drains are these things that most&nbsp;
people have, they need some help remembering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:35.360
And what I've learned is that I can interview&nbsp;
somebody about, so tell me about this last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:39.600
job. Tell me about one of the greatest moments&nbsp;
you had at that job. And we have an interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:44.000
guide in the middle of it, but ultimately&nbsp;
it's about taking time to reflect on it.&nbsp;

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:48.360
So what I've learned is that if I let people&nbsp;
do it over a two-week period or so, they start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:53.360
to remember things like I say, go for a walk and&nbsp;
just think about that job you had back then. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:56.960
they'll go like, "Oh, I remember these people.&nbsp;
I love working with these people." All right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.960 --> 00:20:01.600
well, what was it about that? And so it's this&nbsp;
reflective nature of pulling out those things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:07.280
that give you energy because if you're in a place&nbsp;
that gives you energy, again, it doesn't feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:11.600
you're working. And so it's really important to&nbsp;
find these moments of both energy drivers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:17.400
energy drains to create the requirements of what&nbsp;
you're trying to look for. Because in hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:22.720
the fact is those are the aspects of what&nbsp;
almost like your DNA is wired this way.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:27.080
In the book you have all these nine steps,&nbsp;
all the details. You talked about how maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:30.760
give it two weeks to let it all bubble up.&nbsp;
For folks that maybe just are listening to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:36.360
this and want to do a quick thought exercise at&nbsp;
least or something in the next day to help them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:40.520
figure out a little bit of what gives them&nbsp;
energy, not what do you suggest they do?

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:44.360
I was coaching somebody the other day and&nbsp;
basically they came to me and said, I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:49.480
hate my job. I just really want to quit. I'm like,&nbsp;
okay, but let me ask this. In the last 12 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:56.160
can you think of a time where you literally&nbsp;
enjoyed the moment or two? And they're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.120
yeah, I have a couple of those. And you start&nbsp;
to write them down and then you say, all right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.120 --> 00:21:02.160
well tell me the things that really suck your&nbsp;
energy. And they write them down and then they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:06.320
think about another thing that was basically a&nbsp;
moment, but it's about capturing these moments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:11.520
And most people, they think their job is supposed&nbsp;
to do everything for them and the reality is it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:15.240
not. And so part of it's being able to help&nbsp;
them balance that out and realize that even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:18.760
in a situation that you think is horrible,&nbsp;
there's a lot of things you're learning in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:22.280
the middle of it and it's important to&nbsp;
realize and understand what they are.&nbsp;

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:27.000
And so I would tell people just to take the&nbsp;
time to reflect and say, think about two or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:32.960
three meetings, think about two or three projects,&nbsp;
two or three things that you did where literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:37.720
when you went into it, you actually had an X&nbsp;
amount of energy and when you came out of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:43.480
you actually had 2X, 3X, four x of energy and&nbsp;
say, what was it about that project or that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:48.960
meeting or that team that gave you so much&nbsp;
energy and to be able to make it explicit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:53.880
So actually that becomes a design requirement&nbsp;
for your next job. At the same time we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:59.560
to talk about things you suck at. I always talk&nbsp;
about strength finders and I tell people to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:02.040
I want to know the bottom five.
And they're like, why? It's because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:06.280
those are the five things you really suck at&nbsp;
that you don't even know you suck at. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:11.800
typically where the energy drains come from. And&nbsp;
so it's basically having them look back and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:16.160
where are those moments where? And ultimately&nbsp;
now dissecting, why does it suck? Does it suck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:19.480
because you don't know? Does it suck because&nbsp;
you've done it a thousand times? Does it suck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:24.400
because it's not the right culture? What are the&nbsp;
things that actually make it suck? So you can come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:29.720
up with another set of design requirements.&nbsp;
So ultimately I'm treating you as a product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:35.360
to understand what are your requirements&nbsp;
basically to be able to make progress.

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:40.200
That resonates. What are some examples&nbsp;
of drains and energizers that you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:44.960
come across often just for people to have a&nbsp;
little mental model of what to think about?

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:50.520
So, for example, again, somebody I was coaching,&nbsp;
they love to learn and they realize that the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:55.280
is once they learn something, it's like it becomes&nbsp;
boring to them. And so this notion of being able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.280 --> 00:23:01.520
to actually learn on a regular basis and have&nbsp;
ongoing things. And so we talked, we'll talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:06.000
prototyping later, but what jobs do you actually&nbsp;
always get new things that you have to learn? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:10.160
so while consulting is one of those like, "Oh,&nbsp;
I've never done consulting." That's interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:15.520
Oh, you could do customer success. Well, that's&nbsp;
not new. I'm like, every customer's different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:18.960
Every customer has a different situation. You&nbsp;
have to learn their situation. "Oh yeah, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:23.640
I can learn that way." So part of it is being&nbsp;
able to actually extract those things about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:31.200
learning is and then being able to then translate&nbsp;
it into what can we do with it. So learning.&nbsp;

00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:36.800
There's one about basically helping others. So&nbsp;
one of mine that gives me energy is what I call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:43.040
a maximizer or an individualizer. Like I really&nbsp;
love to basically help people find their way. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:48.240
so everybody who's ever worked with me or I've&nbsp;
coached in my life, it is about me being able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:53.000
to figure out who they are and where can they&nbsp;
go. And so this book is a natural extension of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:58.200
that skill that I have and that I like to&nbsp;
do. And so it's not surprising I got here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:03.280
but I never would've guessed that I would've ended&nbsp;
up in the HR space trying to help people find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.280 --> 00:24:10.800
work. So drains can be everything from some&nbsp;
people love the routine and gives them energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:15.800
and other people hate the routine. It actually&nbsp;
sucks their energy. And so you start to realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:20.040
this is the part of building a team is that&nbsp;
when I start to realize the things that drain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:25.040
my energy and I suck at, I should actually find&nbsp;
my teammates that actually love to do the stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:30.920
I suck at or love the stuff that drains my energy.
Because ultimately that's the diversity of a team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:35.680
that actually makes it really work. And so instead&nbsp;
of trying to, most people try to hire people like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:40.320
themselves, and that actually is where it goes&nbsp;
wrong because then you end up with a very large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:44.720
blind spot where ultimately if you start to&nbsp;
realize what you're good at, what you suck at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:48.760
what gives you energy, what sucks your energy&nbsp;
and start to really complement it with other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:54.680
people. So my business partner of 25 years&nbsp;
is my exact opposite. What I love to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.680 --> 00:25:00.200
he hates to do what he loves to do. I hate to do&nbsp;
all practical purposes, we should not get along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:05.800
but he's my best friend. And ultimately the fact&nbsp;
is we trust each other enough so he knows what not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:09.800
to give me. And when something comes on my plate&nbsp;
that he knows that is really hard for me to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:13.080
he'll take it off and say, let me give you a&nbsp;
draft and you can look at this. I'm like, oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:16.760
perfect. And so these are the kinds of&nbsp;
things you really start to think about.

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:18.720
Many people listen to this&nbsp;
might be like, okay, great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:25.800
I'm going to find that my energizers are check&nbsp;
Twitter all day, go to the beach. And it's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:30.320
how can I find a job that is the energizer&nbsp;
and there's not drains and all these things?

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:34.960
So this is where you have to go... This is where&nbsp;
you have to abstract it beyond the beach. Why do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:39.400
you like to go to the beach? This is where you&nbsp;
have to go. So there's three layers of language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:43.760
I talk about. One layer is the problem layer.&nbsp;
I love to go to the beach. It's like, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:46.920
but you have to understand, well, why do you love&nbsp;
to go to the beach? Oh, I love the sun, I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:51.600
the waves. I'm like, okay, but when you go to the&nbsp;
beach, where do you get? Tell me about a day with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:54.880
the beach where you got energy and tell me about&nbsp;
a day at the beach where you didn't get energy.&nbsp;

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:59.200
It's like, oh, and there's a lot of people&nbsp;
around, oh, I like to be around people. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:03.920
it's about abstracting it down to a level of&nbsp;
causation so you understand what causes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:09.720
beach to be a fun place. And most people just stay&nbsp;
up at the problem layer and they don't really dig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:14.440
deep enough to understand what causes it to say&nbsp;
why they like the beach. And so we talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:19.640
that in the book in terms of how do we unpack the&nbsp;
language so you understand the causal mechanisms.

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:22.840
Another technique that I found helpful&nbsp;
because actually this idea of figuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.840 --> 00:26:27.200
out what energizes you and drains you comes&nbsp;
up often on this podcast, and it was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:31.440
really important for me when I was left my&nbsp;
job to figure out what I wanted to do next.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:37.280
The technique I found really helpful is day to day&nbsp;
pay attention after every meeting and interaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:41.880
did this energize me and did this drain me? And&nbsp;
then you start to detect and then spend more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:46.240
time on the things that energize you and less&nbsp;
time things that drain you as much as you can.

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:50.160
That's right. And so one of the things I'm&nbsp;
trying to teach people now is to use AI to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:54.720
help them with the energy drains because&nbsp;
most people, the one thing I've learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:58.440
is that I grew up as an engineer and as&nbsp;
an engineer was like everything should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:02.840
be a process. But what I've learned is if I&nbsp;
wrap a process around something I love to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:08.320
I actually ruin it. But if I wrap a process about&nbsp;
something I really hate to do and gamify it, I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.320 --> 00:27:12.760
actually get through it. And so lots of times you&nbsp;
start to realize there's these little tricks you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:17.960
learn along the way that help you do that. But&nbsp;
I would say paying attention in your day-to-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:24.600
life about just reflect on the day and say, where&nbsp;
did I get energy today and where did my energy get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:29.640
drained? Will just help you start to articulate&nbsp;
those things because when it comes down to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:35.760
here's the craziest part to me of... One of the&nbsp;
crazy parts, is the job descriptions are made up.&nbsp;

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:40.480
They're literally just made up. And there are&nbsp;
a list of stuff that the manager will say, all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:43.200
right, we want them to do this. And then they'll&nbsp;
think of all the stuff they don't want to do and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:46.840
they put that in there. And so the reality is if&nbsp;
you actually start to look at it and say like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:51.480
hey, I can do these 15 things, but there's these&nbsp;
five things that will literally take all my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:56.200
energy. Is there any way we can think about where&nbsp;
I get more of the stuff I can do versus the stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.200 --> 00:28:01.640
that I really suck at? And most people don't want&nbsp;
to say it, but when they actually do it's amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.640 --> 00:28:05.800
because people go like, oh yeah, I get that.&nbsp;
Okay, we can actually give this off to somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:13.160
else. It's crazy. So this is the other part is I&nbsp;
look at the industry and they've tried to automate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:19.960
the resumes, which is it's all the stuff you did.
It's not the stuff you want to do. And then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:24.480
end up matching it to a resume or to a job&nbsp;
description, which is like a unicorn. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:29.640
trying to find a unicorn, and then ultimately&nbsp;
we're trying to get people to fit the job. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:33.440
you start to realize that nobody fits that job&nbsp;
perfectly and there's too many trade-offs. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:38.480
if you actually reframe this and say, how do I&nbsp;
get the job to fit the person? And you start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:43.360
realize I can change the design of the job and&nbsp;
now they love what they do, they're never going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:48.680
to leave. And so you start to realize it's&nbsp;
about actually understanding how to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:52.840
which is I think really, really powerful. And I&nbsp;
have some companies that are starting to do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:58.280
and the results are crazy, productivities through&nbsp;
the roof, all these different crazy things.

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:02.240
I definitely want to come back to that when we&nbsp;
talk about finding and keeping awesome people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:08.760
And that's a great foreshadowing of that.&nbsp;
I wanted to double down on the importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:15.680
of this discussion of finding energy drivers and&nbsp;
drains. Because going back to the first question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:24.200
asked of the difference between job features and&nbsp;
job experiences is my sense is understanding what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:30.200
energizes you will help you find a job, will&nbsp;
help you overcome these features of a fancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.200 --> 00:29:35.800
title and a fancy salary and focus more on&nbsp;
the experiences such that you are happier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:40.920
and thrive at this new job and love it versus&nbsp;
tricked with this awesome title and salary.

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:46.280
We to map the features to the experiences that&nbsp;
actually make it happen because features are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:51.720
actually static, your job title, but if I get&nbsp;
the title, I can impress others, I can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:56.960
people think, that makes me feel like I'm making&nbsp;
products to go from a director to a VP. Right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.960 --> 00:30:01.200
And so part of it's I actually understand why do&nbsp;
you want that feature and what is it going to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:07.200
for you? So it's this notion of, again, action as&nbsp;
opposed to most features are static, so what does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:11.960
it actually do for you? And what happens through&nbsp;
time is that title will wear down over time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:18.080
so it has a depreciation to it that now I've been&nbsp;
a VP, now I got to be a C-level person. So all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:22.040
a sudden you start to realize that feature,&nbsp;
which is really important in the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:27.640
ends up being a push in the end because it's&nbsp;
like, hey, I haven't gotten promoted in a while.

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:32.320
And then you end up being in that VP role&nbsp;
and what this sucks, what am I doing here?

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:36.480
Yeah, well, so that's the other part is you start&nbsp;
to realize how many people look around. What are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:41.440
the big pushes is when I look around and I don't&nbsp;
want my boss's job and I don't know where to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:46.360
next. And the fact is, what happens is an opening&nbsp;
comes up and they put you into a position that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.360 --> 00:30:50.480
you don't want and then you're like, I don't&nbsp;
really want to do this. And so ultimately a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:54.680
of this starts when they can't see where they&nbsp;
can grow and go. And so it starts to realize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.560 --> 00:31:00.120
and that's where a lot of this starts. And so&nbsp;
letting people understand what they can do and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:05.000
where they can go is really, really important. And&nbsp;
again, we'll get to that, but that's a big one.

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:09.840
I want to come back to somebody looking&nbsp;
for jobs. So they either got let go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:12.400
they left a job, they hid it&nbsp;
and they haven't found some new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.400 --> 00:31:17.080
what are just some tactics you suggest based on&nbsp;
this framework to help them be more successful?

00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:21.720
So the first thing I would say is when you&nbsp;
can distill this down to the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.720 --> 00:31:26.680
give you energy and what you're good at, the&nbsp;
thing that I think has been most powerful is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:32.480
again, treating you like a product. How do we&nbsp;
prototype different job positions for you? So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:35.720
how do we think of you? So I was talking to&nbsp;
somebody or I was coaching somebody who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:41.120
a neuroscientist and they had just come back&nbsp;
from Ireland and they were running this big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:45.800
lab and they got burnt out and they came and&nbsp;
basically took a job at a hospital. And as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.800 --> 00:31:49.840
started to talk about what gave her energy and&nbsp;
not, we said, well, what about being a design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:58.240
researcher? What about being a National Geographic&nbsp;
coordinator? So it's this notion of prototyping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:03.680
wide because what we realize is most people don't&nbsp;
feel like they have agency to go anywhere else.&nbsp;

00:32:03.680 --> 00:32:07.600
And the reality is if you're really good at the&nbsp;
things you're good at, they're used in a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:12.880
other places. And so you'd think, boy, if I'm&nbsp;
in finance or I'm in marketing in a financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:16.160
corporation, oh I got to find another financial&nbsp;
company. But if you're good at marketing and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.160 --> 00:32:21.480
like what you do, you can go to a lot of different&nbsp;
places. And so it's this notion of starting by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:27.480
doing what we call informational interviews to&nbsp;
other jobs that are out there. So it's like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:33.200
was coaching somebody and I said, all we're this&nbsp;
person that was the neuroscientist. It's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:35.880
all right, we're going to go find somebody who's&nbsp;
in that geo coordinator, we're going to go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:39.480
LinkedIn and find somebody who either had the&nbsp;
job or has the job and you're going to interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:45.400
them to say, "What's it like to have this job?"
And this does two things. It gives you practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.400 --> 00:32:49.880
talking about yourself and talking to other&nbsp;
people, which most people haven't done in a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.880 --> 00:32:54.960
time. And the other part is it allows you to start&nbsp;
to put yourself in that situation and go like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:59.920
well, this really worked for me or not. And it&nbsp;
turned out the fact is she was thinking she could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:05.400
travel and she could do science and she could&nbsp;
help people be a teacher. And it turns out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:09.240
geo coordinator is just like a travel agent. It&nbsp;
literally, it's all pre-programmed and everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:15.360
else. And she's like, "Oh, I'm out." And so it is&nbsp;
helping them actually put the rubber to the road&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:20.480
on some of these notions because most people start&nbsp;
applying for jobs, but they really don't know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.480 --> 00:33:26.040
they are. And so what I would tell you is one&nbsp;
is as somebody looking for a job is distill your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:32.120
skills, distill what gives you energy, distill&nbsp;
those things and make sure you're clear on those.&nbsp;

00:33:32.120 --> 00:33:36.880
Go wide and find what many different&nbsp;
industries that can do it. Go talk to friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.880 --> 00:33:42.400
people who have these jobs and start to realize&nbsp;
what they are and narrow down to one area that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.400 --> 00:33:46.000
you really feel like you can actually go&nbsp;
to that's going to give you the outcomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:51.840
that you want. It's this notion of prototyping&nbsp;
very wide to learn and then using it to narrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:57.160
and then basically figure out the real thing you&nbsp;
want to go after and why you want to go after it.

00:33:57.160 --> 00:34:01.200
I love how, again, this relates to a product&nbsp;
where when you're designing the product, you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:06.160
talked to potential customers of this thing and&nbsp;
understand what problems they have and it's a fit.

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:09.880
That's right. Here's the thing is job descriptions&nbsp;
should really be, here's the context we're in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:14.640
Here's what this role is about. Here's what&nbsp;
progress means in this role and here's how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.640 --> 00:34:20.320
will actually reward you for actually doing this&nbsp;
work. And it's just not that way. And so again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:25.120
I'm working on another extension of this book&nbsp;
around just helping companies implement this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.120 --> 00:34:32.280
And so it's so fascinating. It's like it's a&nbsp;
thread that just keeps pulling. I keep pulling.

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:35.720
Speaking of that, so let me try&nbsp;
to describe the framework so far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.720 --> 00:34:38.600
And I know it's not complete in the book&nbsp;
basically walks you through step by step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:43.360
but it's essentially understand what's&nbsp;
pushing you out of your current job. Was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.360 --> 00:34:46.080
it those four quests? Are they pushing you&nbsp;
or they're pulling you? I forget exactly.

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:50.200
They're both. So the thing is there's got&nbsp;
to be a push and then at some point if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.200 --> 00:34:54.480
there's just push and there's no pull, the&nbsp;
reality is that then you're just going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.480 --> 00:34:59.160
about your job. So part of it is the push&nbsp;
actually gives you the energy to look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:02.400
but you have to have the pulls on the other&nbsp;
side to know which direction to go. Think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:06.200
it as like a compass. And so these quests&nbsp;
help you understand, am I going north,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.200 --> 00:35:10.680
am I going south? Am I going east? Am I going&nbsp;
west? Do I need control? Do I need alignment?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.680 --> 00:35:14.720
Do I need to get out? Do I need to do it? Take&nbsp;
the next step. And ultimately based on that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:21.800
that's going to shape how we actually then load&nbsp;
your strengths, your energy drivers, your energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.800 --> 00:35:26.320
drains as air, how are we going to aim this&nbsp;
thing? So it's about aiming where you go next.

00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:29.000
Awesome. That's exactly where I was going&nbsp;
to go. So essentially it's figure out which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:32.600
of these things are pushing and pulling. You&nbsp;
figure out what energizes you and drains you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.600 --> 00:35:38.080
to come up with a checklist of here's what&nbsp;
I want my next role to be. And then you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.080 --> 00:35:43.960
this prototyping where you interview people, you&nbsp;
make a broad list of potential places and jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:48.800
and then interview them about what that life&nbsp;
is like to see which checkboxes are checked.

00:35:48.800 --> 00:35:54.000
Yep. And ultimately the biggest thing is&nbsp;
about the trade-offs you have to make. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:59.040
job is perfect and ultimately people are looking&nbsp;
for the thing that checks all the boxes and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:04.560
start to realize nothing checks all the boxes. So&nbsp;
what are you willing to give up to get? And so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:09.920
was coaching somebody a while ago who basically&nbsp;
was an entrepreneur and he was at one company and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:13.680
he had been there for five years. They had&nbsp;
gone from basically being, I'll say nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:17.720
to basically being over a hundred million,&nbsp;
but it wasn't small anymore. And he's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.720 --> 00:36:21.920
I want to be a founder someday, so I want to take&nbsp;
the next step. But the reality is like I think I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.920 --> 00:36:26.520
want to go work for one more entrepreneur. And&nbsp;
so they basically went for, he got four or five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:33.240
job offers he could make. He got one being an&nbsp;
engineer somewhere and it was paying 3 50, 400.&nbsp;

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:37.720
And he had another job where he could actually&nbsp;
work next to as almost like the chief of staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.720 --> 00:36:42.600
of a very well-known entrepreneur. And he&nbsp;
would learn a lot. And so the question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:49.360
and it was like 200 and which one do you want? And&nbsp;
he ended up taking the job with the entrepreneur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:49.360 --> 00:36:53.240
to teach him, but he actually went in and&nbsp;
said, I'm taking this job, I have this other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:53.240 --> 00:36:58.320
job for this other money. I'm giving up this much&nbsp;
money so I can learn from you. And he said, fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:02.520
I'll make you chief of staff. And ultimately he&nbsp;
said, and you're here for two years so you can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:07.440
a founder. So they actually reframed the job to&nbsp;
literally fit him. And then what I told him is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:11.120
you can't go back and bitch about the money&nbsp;
because you made the trade-off to do it.&nbsp;

00:37:11.120 --> 00:37:14.440
So you can't go like, "Oh God, I should get&nbsp;
more money." It's like, this is how it is and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:20.440
this is a choice you made live with it, but put&nbsp;
a timeframe on it. And so it's these trade-offs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:25.720
that are really, really important and actually&nbsp;
landing the job because most people want it all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:31.120
And I mean if you look back on your career,&nbsp;
you never got it all. And so the other part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:31.120 --> 00:37:36.240
is to realize a side gig or a hobby or these other&nbsp;
things can actually supplement some of those other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.240 --> 00:37:42.880
energy drivers. You get from basically the job&nbsp;
that you can't get everything from the same job.

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:49.760
Does it sometimes make sense to optimize&nbsp;
for the features, the salary, the title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.760 --> 00:37:54.200
or is that generally just a bad idea versus&nbsp;
the experiences versus these energizers?

00:37:54.200 --> 00:38:01.520
What I've realized, and this is another insight,&nbsp;
is I can actually, and this is a bad way to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:06.120
at it, but I can actually pay people less if I&nbsp;
give them better experiences. And so ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:10.960
I can actually do more and then I just don't&nbsp;
count on them staying so long. And so for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:14.720
I actually pay people, I pay them fairly&nbsp;
well, but I know they can make more money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.720 --> 00:38:18.720
elsewhere. And ultimately I want them to be&nbsp;
attracted to go. So if they need more money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:22.560
they should go somewhere else. But if they're&nbsp;
here to learn, that's what I want people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:27.720
are here to learn. And basically the way I&nbsp;
work it is I give them a reasonable salary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:31.920
and then I give them big bonuses so they can&nbsp;
save money to go do what they want to go do.&nbsp;

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:36.480
So I always see this as, I don't expect anybody&nbsp;
to be with me though. People have been with me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:40.160
for a long time. It's one of those things where&nbsp;
every year we sit down and talk about what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.160 --> 00:38:46.320
progress mean to you and how do we actually&nbsp;
figure it out? And ultimately, I've added some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:50.760
offerings to my business that I would never do,&nbsp;
but I know that this other person who works for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.760 --> 00:38:56.040
me wanted to actually do more coaching. And I'm&nbsp;
like, okay, we brought in three coaching clients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.040 --> 00:39:00.080
and basically she loved it and that gave her&nbsp;
basically more energy. And so part of it is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:05.720
make sure that I can adapt to basically keep her&nbsp;
here and make sure that she's making progress.

00:39:05.720 --> 00:39:07.600
I don't think there's anything&nbsp;
controversial about that. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:10.920
you're a cool company that everyone&nbsp;
wants to work at, people will pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:15.066
will take less salary because of the experience&nbsp;
they're going to get and the potential.

00:39:15.066 --> 00:39:19.760
That's right. And the other problem I&nbsp;
realized is that when you overpay people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:19.760 --> 00:39:23.800
what happens is that they actually become&nbsp;
more and more scared that they will lose it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:27.000
and then they become more and more conservative&nbsp;
because they don't want to rock the boat and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:31.720
actually don't work. They do what they're told&nbsp;
as opposed to do what they should do. And so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:38.080
start to realize that money has a very interesting&nbsp;
impact on behavior, and I have not studied it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.080 --> 00:39:42.720
any great fashion. There's many people who have&nbsp;
studied more than me, but that observation of when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:46.920
I overpay people, they're all about, I just don't&nbsp;
want to not get my bonus and you better make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:50.840
that I can. And you start to realize, but they&nbsp;
don't know what it takes to get the bonus. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

