we got to go through the process. And so basically&nbsp;
they have to come to me and tell me their energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:57.400 --> 01:07:00.320
drivers and drains. They have to tell me what&nbsp;
they're good at and what they suck at. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:00.320 --> 01:07:06.240
have to be able to tell me their past couple&nbsp;
jobs of what's going on. And so you start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:06.240 --> 01:07:10.360
realize from a hiring perspective, you start to&nbsp;
realize the other thing is to realize that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:10.360 --> 01:07:15.160
job description is made up and that you should&nbsp;
be trying to actually match the job to fit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:15.160 --> 01:07:20.040
person as opposed to trying to find the person to&nbsp;
fit the job. Because when you find... As you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:20.040 --> 01:07:23.520
you've been in a startup, it's about good&nbsp;
people. And when you find good people, it's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:23.520 --> 01:07:28.000
yeah, they fit, but they're just not perfect.
And then you let them go. And the reality is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:28.000 --> 01:07:32.040
you probably want to do is actually find a way to&nbsp;
get that person in and figure out how to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:32.040 --> 01:07:38.080
reshape the job to fit that person. And you start&nbsp;
to realize that's really the key to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:38.080 --> 01:07:43.280
it. And what I would tell you is the other thing&nbsp;
is to think about writing the job description as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:43.280 --> 01:07:47.920
a set of experiences that people can have. It's&nbsp;
almost like you need to think of the job though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:47.920 --> 01:07:52.760
I know that there's a legal part of all this. You&nbsp;
got to think about marketing to people who want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:52.760 --> 01:07:57.560
do this. And so my whole thing is most people&nbsp;
wait for people to raise their hand. They've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:57.560 --> 01:08:02.040
already left the job. My belief is there's a lot&nbsp;
of people who would do a new job if you actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:02.040 --> 01:08:06.280
wrote the job description away that says, hey,&nbsp;
come here and learn this and do these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:06.280 --> 01:08:12.240
and be able to work in a team like this way and&nbsp;
help them understand what they're trying to do.&nbsp;

01:08:12.240 --> 01:08:16.920
I think that that's the two bigger things&nbsp;
is fix the job descriptions and build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:16.920 --> 01:08:21.120
process that helps you understand people's&nbsp;
energy drivers and drains and helping people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:21.120 --> 01:08:25.440
be more transparent about when somebody&nbsp;
says, what do you suck at? It's like, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:26.120 --> 01:08:32.600
I work too much." That's just a first clue to&nbsp;
me. You don't know really what you really suck&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:32.600 --> 01:08:39.040
at because everybody sucks at something and you&nbsp;
haven't done the homework to do it. And so to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:39.040 --> 01:08:46.000
the people who are recruiting who have read this&nbsp;
basically have used it to say, use the forces for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:46.000 --> 01:08:50.080
example, to say, why are you leaving your job?&nbsp;
What's going on? And then ultimately, what are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:50.080 --> 01:08:53.600
you looking for in the next job? And so they can&nbsp;
use the pushes and pulls as a way to start the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:53.600 --> 01:08:58.440
conversation around the interview process. Right?
So there's a lot of different ways where this can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:58.440 --> 01:09:04.440
be put in, but let's be clear, I wrote this,&nbsp;
we wrote this, Michael Horn and Ethan Bernstein&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:04.440 --> 01:09:09.840
and I, we wrote this with the intent of helping&nbsp;
employees hire companies. And ultimately we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:09.840 --> 01:09:14.200
a lot of companies coming back to us and saying,&nbsp;
help us fix the way we recruit, help us fix the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:14.200 --> 01:09:21.800
way we write job descriptions, help us with the&nbsp;
way in which we do performance reviews. Ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:22.440 --> 01:09:26.800
we've got to align the company's progress&nbsp;
with their progress because if they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:26.800 --> 01:09:31.360
make progress, they will leave. And so that's&nbsp;
the other part of this is to actually start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:31.360 --> 01:09:36.520
think about some of the concepts in here and how&nbsp;
does it affect the way you actually manage people.

01:09:36.520 --> 01:09:39.800
So say someone's hiring right now,&nbsp;
they have a job description. What's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:39.800 --> 01:09:43.160
something they can do to improve&nbsp;
the odds of finding someone awesome?

01:09:43.160 --> 01:09:47.280
Yeah. So the first thing I would do is I would&nbsp;
actually look at the job description and unpack&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:47.280 --> 01:09:51.280
what you mean by things. Get it down to what are&nbsp;
the... So, for example, they'll say five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:51.280 --> 01:09:55.320
experience. What I will tell you is that's one&nbsp;
of the worst statements you can put on any job&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:56.200 --> 01:10:01.960
like recruiting thing because why five years?&nbsp;
What is it about? What this to me is a sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:01.960 --> 01:10:05.600
that you're lazy because you're saying, oh, you&nbsp;
need to have five years experience. For an entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:05.600 --> 01:10:10.880
entry-level job, how does that work? What do&nbsp;
you really need to know? And so my thing is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:10.880 --> 01:10:15.200
what does somebody who has five-year experience&nbsp;
have and be more specific about what it is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:15.200 --> 01:10:18.120
you can actually understand because there&nbsp;
might be somebody with three years experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:18.120 --> 01:10:22.360
who's perfect, but they're not even going to&nbsp;
apply because you put five years on there.&nbsp;

01:10:22.360 --> 01:10:26.480
And so there's all these kinds of things. So look&nbsp;
at the way you've written the job description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:26.480 --> 01:10:32.320
look at the way you've wrote the requirements&nbsp;
and be more specific. It's like, yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:32.320 --> 01:10:38.240
you need to know Excel, PowerPoint and word,&nbsp;
why? What do you do with it? Tell me what I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:38.240 --> 01:10:42.000
going to do with those. Don't tell me I need&nbsp;
the skills in that. Tell me you're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:42.000 --> 01:10:45.560
need to be able to build PowerPoints&nbsp;
and do things around this, which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:45.560 --> 01:10:51.280
you need to know these things. Tell them what&nbsp;
they're going to do as opposed to what it is.

01:10:51.280 --> 01:10:53.520
Experiences. Essentially going&nbsp;
back to the very first question.

01:10:53.520 --> 01:10:57.200
Experiences. Back to experiences and features&nbsp;
like you're asking for features of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:57.200 --> 01:11:02.160
five years experience, you've got an MBA. Those&nbsp;
are all features of people. Talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:02.160 --> 01:11:06.240
experiences you want people to have to come into&nbsp;
your company. This is what happens when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:06.240 --> 01:11:14.480
an outsider looking at an industry that's just...&nbsp;
At some point, it makes very little sense to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.480 --> 01:11:21.080
right? The HR department, it is very unknown to&nbsp;
me. I could never get a job. So this is the funny&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:21.080 --> 01:11:25.040
part as people ask me, when did you want to be an&nbsp;
entrepreneur? I said, the moment that I couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:25.040 --> 01:11:29.920
get a job because as a dyslexic you couldn't&nbsp;
even write a resume that anybody could see.

01:11:29.920 --> 01:11:34.920
For people that didn't listen to the last episode&nbsp;
and learn that you're actually super dyslexic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:34.920 --> 01:11:40.200
and can't really read or write talk, what's&nbsp;
the extent of that for people to understand?

01:11:40.200 --> 01:11:45.240
What I believe is that I had three close-head&nbsp;
brain injuries before I was seven years old. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:45.240 --> 01:11:50.920
belief is some of it was there beforehand, some&nbsp;
of it wasn't. But the reality is I blame it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:50.920 --> 01:11:58.160
my stupid things I did as a kid. But the reality&nbsp;
is by the time I was 18 years old, I had a third&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:58.160 --> 01:12:06.920
grade reading level. I have ADHD, I am very neuro&nbsp;
typical. And ultimately... But it turned out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:06.920 --> 01:12:12.240
the only way my mom taught me how I learned and&nbsp;
she literally, for example, she'd have me circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:12.240 --> 01:12:18.360
the five largest words in a paragraph. I could see&nbsp;
words that were seven letters or longer and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:18.360 --> 01:12:22.680
she'd have me guess why those five words would be&nbsp;
together. And that's how I learned how to read.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:24.240 --> 01:12:28.240
But I could memorize the five words from the first&nbsp;
paragraph to the last paragraph, and so I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:28.240 --> 01:12:32.600
turn through a book and very quickly get a very&nbsp;
good understanding of what the book was about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:32.600 --> 01:12:37.640
but I can't read it like everybody else reads&nbsp;
it in terms of small words and everything else.&nbsp;

01:12:37.640 --> 01:12:43.480
But ultimately what that did is that disability&nbsp;
created super ability in me, which is questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:43.480 --> 01:12:49.160
I know how to ask so many questions because that's&nbsp;
the way I learn. And so at some point in time, my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.160 --> 01:12:53.800
disability has caused me to have super abilities.&nbsp;
That's why I think your weaknesses actually create&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:53.800 --> 01:12:58.600
your super abilities and knowing what they are is&nbsp;
so important. And so this is where I think a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.600 --> 01:13:03.680
of this comes from is to realize I was supposed&nbsp;
to be a baggage handler or a construction worker.&nbsp;

01:13:03.680 --> 01:13:07.920
And my mom basically told me is like, if you&nbsp;
understand how you learn and what you do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:07.920 --> 01:13:11.600
you can do it, but you have to be careful&nbsp;
because if you get labeled as dyslexic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:11.600 --> 01:13:16.720
you will be basically seen as special needs.&nbsp;
And this is 19, when I got first labeled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:16.720 --> 01:13:21.120
I was put into special needs classes, which was a&nbsp;
room at the end of the hall. You just sat in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:21.120 --> 01:13:25.680
day and didn't really learn anything because there&nbsp;
was no real programs for any of it. So she taught&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:25.680 --> 01:13:32.480
me how to really school and learn my own way.&nbsp;
And that's where I think I built my superpowers.

01:13:32.480 --> 01:13:40.600
Wow. I feel like I could do a whole episode&nbsp;
deep dive into this. I just had Tobias Lütke&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:40.600 --> 01:13:44.720
on the podcast, the CEO of Shopify, he's also&nbsp;
dyslexic. And we actually didn't get that.

01:13:44.720 --> 01:13:50.240
There's an amazing amount of entrepreneurs&nbsp;
who are dyslexic. And I attribute it back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:50.240 --> 01:13:54.600
the fact it's because we couldn't&nbsp;
get jobs. We just went to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:54.600 --> 01:13:58.920
our own thing because we had to&nbsp;
figure out our own way to work.

01:13:58.920 --> 01:14:03.160
Yeah, that's a bittersweet feature maybe.

01:14:03.160 --> 01:14:10.120
Yeah. I think that... Whenever I meet somebody&nbsp;
with a disability and they talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:10.120 --> 01:14:15.440
disability, I quickly go, "What's your super&nbsp;
ability?" Tell me what it's because somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:15.440 --> 01:14:19.720
who's blind can hear things that are just&nbsp;
unbelievable. They can smell things. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:19.720 --> 01:14:23.800
can smell people walking in the room, just&nbsp;
crazy, crazy stuff. And you start to realize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.800 --> 01:14:27.520
I know that you don't have sight, but the&nbsp;
reality is it's actually affected you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:27.520 --> 01:14:31.960
another way. And what does that superpower and&nbsp;
how do we actually leverage that superpower?

01:14:31.960 --> 01:14:36.720
Okay. I want to touch on two more things&nbsp;
before I let you go that I think are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:36.720 --> 01:14:39.720
to be helpful to a lot of people. One&nbsp;
is being a founder, the other is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:39.720 --> 01:14:44.600
getting into alignment with what you're doing in&nbsp;
life. So from the perspective being a founder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:44.600 --> 01:14:48.600
you told me that people actually can&nbsp;
use this framework we've been talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:48.600 --> 01:14:54.520
about to decide if they should even be&nbsp;
founders. What is your advice there?

01:14:54.520 --> 01:14:59.600
Part of it is to realize as a founder, you have&nbsp;
to do a lot of different things. And so part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:59.600 --> 01:15:06.080
it is I think self-awareness is one of the biggest&nbsp;
assets you're going to have. And to know what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:06.080 --> 01:15:09.600
you're good at, to know what you suck at, it's&nbsp;
also going to tell you about the team. You need&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:09.600 --> 01:15:13.760
to build around you. And so to realize at some&nbsp;
point, there's a lot of people who say they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:13.760 --> 01:15:18.200
to be a founder, but what I would tell you is&nbsp;
when you start to realize what you're good at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.200 --> 01:15:22.320
what's your strengths, what do you&nbsp;
suck at, what are your energy drains?&nbsp;

01:15:22.320 --> 01:15:27.920
And then go talk to a couple of founders, you'll&nbsp;
start to realize how to shape for you to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:27.920 --> 01:15:33.440
a founder and whether for you, you decided&nbsp;
I'm on my own, I'm going to do this myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:33.440 --> 01:15:38.280
and I can hire people on contracting, but I want&nbsp;
no employees. And so you start to realize how do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:38.280 --> 01:15:44.360
you want to shape this thing based on who you are?&nbsp;
And you took the time to do that. But most people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:44.360 --> 01:15:48.600
they have this notion of being a founder, but&nbsp;
they don't know what founder they want to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:48.600 --> 01:15:51.400
And what I would tell you is that there&nbsp;
are a whole bunch of different kinds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:51.400 --> 01:15:58.040
founders and that you should self awareness&nbsp;
and knowing energy drivers and drains and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:58.040 --> 01:16:02.200
the reason why you want to be a founder&nbsp;
become paramount to actually your success.

01:16:02.200 --> 01:16:07.440
And so the advice there is look at your energy&nbsp;
drivers and drains and also talk to founders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:07.440 --> 01:16:11.480
the interview prototype interviews&nbsp;
to understand what it actually is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:11.480 --> 01:16:13.280
and make sure you actually want to be doing that.

01:16:13.280 --> 01:16:19.560
Because what's interesting is I would say, so I've&nbsp;
done two startups in the last year, so this is my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:19.560 --> 01:16:25.160
eighth, and ninth startup. And what I realized is&nbsp;
if I had go back to when I did my first startup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:25.160 --> 01:16:30.640
I spent more time on the logo and the website&nbsp;
and all these other things that literally both&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:30.640 --> 01:16:34.680
of them, neither one has a website yet. It doesn't&nbsp;
matter, right? I'm building the product, I got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.680 --> 01:16:38.720
to make it a short works. And so you start to&nbsp;
realize, you just realize there's different things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:38.720 --> 01:16:43.840
you need to do. And what happens is you confuse&nbsp;
activity with productivity and you end up doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:43.840 --> 01:16:48.640
a lot of work that just doesn't matter and you're&nbsp;
stressing yourself out for all the wrong reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:48.640 --> 01:16:53.280
And so part of it is getting to be self-aware&nbsp;
of those kinds of things is really important.

01:16:53.280 --> 01:16:58.200
Speaking of being self-aware, last&nbsp;
question is around getting back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:58.200 --> 01:17:02.040
alignment with yourself. You also&nbsp;
tell me that people use this to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:02.040 --> 01:17:05.600
with overwhelm and understanding&nbsp;
and solve their career course.

01:17:05.600 --> 01:17:09.800
I didn't build it for this reason, I didn't&nbsp;
build it for this reason, but there was a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:09.800 --> 01:17:17.440
in November where I was just overwhelmed. I was&nbsp;
literally like, come on, this is not what I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:17.440 --> 01:17:23.800
to do. This is, and there's just so many things&nbsp;
pulling on me and pressing on me that I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:23.800 --> 01:17:27.640
okay. And I like, you know what? I'm going to&nbsp;
go take the test. So when job moves, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:27.640 --> 01:17:31.400
some resources and there's a test you can take and&nbsp;
it'll tell you which quest you're in. So it asks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:31.400 --> 01:17:35.880
you pushes and pulls and puts it together and&nbsp;
then tells you the probability that you're in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:35.880 --> 01:17:42.680
one of the four quests. And as I did it, I went&nbsp;
through it and just said, this is where I'm at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:42.680 --> 01:17:45.960
this what's going on? This is what's pushing me,&nbsp;
this what's pulling me, where should I go next?&nbsp;

01:17:45.960 --> 01:17:51.480
And it says, you need to have realignment. So&nbsp;
basically it was a realignment job and I realized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:51.480 --> 01:17:55.400
okay, what are the five things that are really&nbsp;
pulling me out of alignment? It was like all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:55.400 --> 01:17:59.840
podcasts I had to do. I wasn't building product,&nbsp;
I was promoting more. I was doing all these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:59.840 --> 01:18:06.400
that I had to figure out how to sell books to big&nbsp;
companies, all this stuff that just isn't me. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:06.400 --> 01:18:10.760
I realized, screw it. I'm going to buy the books.&nbsp;
I'm going to give them away. I'm going to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:10.760 --> 01:18:16.200
I was able to actually look at that list and pull&nbsp;
the things off my list and basically either not do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:16.200 --> 01:18:21.960
them or delegate them to somebody else. And it&nbsp;
was actually about me pulling myself back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:21.960 --> 01:18:25.440
alignment. So I actually had more energy,&nbsp;
and to be honest, I woke up the next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:26.400 --> 01:18:30.360
I was a young entrepreneur again.
And so it's this aspect of realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:30.360 --> 01:18:34.200
why do I want to get out? Why do I want to take&nbsp;
the next step? What is the next step? Well, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:34.200 --> 01:18:38.760
how do I frame the next step and take it? And so&nbsp;
to be honest, I'm now using it as almost like a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:38.760 --> 01:18:44.480
not daily, but once a month I'll take it just to&nbsp;
actually help me realize what's going on. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:44.480 --> 01:18:50.160
helped me stop the energy that's pulling me in&nbsp;
one direction or another and helps me stay on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:50.160 --> 01:18:55.920
track. And it's just a really cool tool to do&nbsp;
that. And so I have a couple of colleagues who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:55.920 --> 01:19:00.920
are doing it as well, and it just is interesting&nbsp;
how they realize I'm not a very good delegator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:00.920 --> 01:19:05.720
but when I realized it affects my motivation this&nbsp;
way, it's like all of a sudden I've become a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:05.720 --> 01:19:10.760
better delegator to say, these are the things&nbsp;
that are just pulling me or are misaligning me.

01:19:10.760 --> 01:19:14.000
Wow, that is so funny. Okay, so I know&nbsp;
you finished the book in November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:14.000 --> 01:19:16.560
so I imagine that was related to your role.

01:19:16.560 --> 01:19:20.320
It was all about the law to the book. And&nbsp;
it's like my whole thing is that the book&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:20.320 --> 01:19:26.480
was done and it was like then the publisher put&nbsp;
all this... They put so much pressure on you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:26.480 --> 01:19:30.960
do all these other things and they have no data&nbsp;
to tell you how well the book's going to do or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:30.960 --> 01:19:34.960
not going to do it. And I'm like, it's done.&nbsp;
Just get it out there. And they're like, no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:34.960 --> 01:19:39.040
no, no. We got to presale. We got to do this.&nbsp;
You got to do all this promotion. And I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:39.040 --> 01:19:47.120
"Thank God I had two other authors to help me with&nbsp;
that load." But I mean, we had an article in HBR,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:47.120 --> 01:19:53.240
had the idea cast, they were on Wall Street&nbsp;
Journal. I don't know any of that stuff. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.240 --> 01:19:57.960
get intimidated by all that stuff. So it's like&nbsp;
like, okay, you guys take that stuff. But still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:58.480 --> 01:20:04.200
I had to do a lot of different speaking. And&nbsp;
I love to do this because I know the people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:04.200 --> 01:20:10.160
I know your audience, I know what to do, but&nbsp;
talking about HR to HR people is scary to me.

01:20:11.200 --> 01:20:14.400
I don't think they would be happy with&nbsp;
what you've shared today necessarily.

01:20:14.400 --> 01:20:17.080
Yeah. I'm sure we're going to get some, just&nbsp;
so you're clear, we're probably going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:17.080 --> 01:20:21.360
some backlash from some of it as well. But&nbsp;
again, I am looking at it as I am trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:21.360 --> 01:20:25.760
